	<a href="/events/">Events</a>
	<span aria-hidden="true">•</span>
	<a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
</nav>

<style>
	/* The site's navigation. One call site, src/_components/header.webc, so the
	   four parameters it used to take are inlined: a `webc:setup` block emulated
	   default attribute values and concatenated them into a `:style` literal that
	   the rules read back with their own fallbacks, for values whose one caller
	   passed two of them the defaults verbatim and never passed the other two.
	   Worse than redundant: `webc:setup` cannot see a component's attributes at
	   all, so `nav-label="Main navigation"` was resolving to the "Navigation"
	   default and no caller could ever have changed it. (A `:`/`@` expression in
	   the markup *can* read attributes — postcard-panel.webc and event-card.webc
	   do. Only `webc:setup` cannot.) A second navigation wanting different type
	   is the moment to add a parameter, and then it will have two callers.

	   In the stylesheet rather than an HTML comment above the markup: the
	   <style> block is emitted once per page, the markup once per render. */

	.nav-links {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-xs);
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		text-transform: uppercase;
	}

	.nav-links a {
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--antiflash-white);
		font-size: var(--step-0);
	}

	.nav-links a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-color: var(--battleship-gray);
		text-underline-offset: 2px;
	}

	.nav-links span {
		color: var(--battleship-gray);
	}
</style>
